 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "residential-roofing-cost-in-auckland-an-honest-guide-for-homeowners",
   "title": "Residential Roofing Cost in Auckland: An Honest Guide for Homeowners",
   "date": "2026-05-02",
   "image": "blog-roofing-cost.webp",
   "excerpt": "One of the first questions Auckland homeowners ask when they start thinking about their roof is how much it is going to cost. It is a completely reasonable question and one that does not have a simple answer because residential",
   "content": "<p>One of the first questions Auckland homeowners ask when they start thinking about their roof is how much it is going to cost. It is a completely reasonable question and one that does not have a simple answer because residential roofing costs in Auckland vary significantly depending on a range of factors specific to your home and your situation.</p>\n<p>This guide breaks down everything that affects residential roofing costs in Auckland, what realistic price ranges look like for different types of projects and what to consider before you start getting quotes so you can approach the process with confidence and clarity.</p>\n<h3><b>Why Residential Roofing Costs in Auckland Vary So Much</b></h3>\n<p>If you have already started researching roofing costs you have probably noticed that the numbers vary enormously. Two homes of a similar size on the same street can receive quotes that differ by tens of thousands of dollars and understanding why this happens is important before you start comparing numbers.</p>\n<p>The size of your roof is the most fundamental variable. Residential roofing in Auckland is priced per square metre so a larger roof will always cost more to replace than a smaller one regardless of the material chosen. A compact two bedroom home has considerably less roof area than a large four bedroom property and that difference in size translates directly to a difference in cost.</p>\n<p>The pitch of your roof has a significant impact on the complexity and cost of the work. A low pitch roof is considerably more straightforward to work on than a steeply pitched one. Steep roofs require more safety equipment, take longer to work on safely and demand a higher level of skill from the roofing crew. Every degree of additional pitch adds time and cost to the project.</p>\n<p>The shape of your roof matters just as much as the pitch. A simple gable roof with two planes and minimal flashings is far less complex than a roof with multiple hips, valleys, dormers, skylights and penetrations. Every additional feature adds materials and labour to the job. A complex roof can cost significantly more per square metre than a simple one of the same overall area.</p>\n<p>The condition of the underlying roof structure is something that cannot always be assessed fully until the old roofing material is removed. If the sarking, battens or structural framing are damaged or deteriorating they will need to be repaired or replaced as part of the project. This is essential work that protects the longevity of the new roof but it does add to the overall cost. A thorough initial assessment by an experienced roofing contractor will identify any obvious structural concerns before the project begins.</p>\n<p>The roofing material you choose is one of the biggest variables in the overall cost of any residential roofing project in Auckland. Different materials have very different price points per square metre and the right choice depends on your home, your budget and the result you want to achieve.</p>\n<p>Replacing flashings, guttering, spouting and fascia at the same time as the roof cladding is best practice and adds to the overall scope of work. These elements deteriorate over time and replacing them while the roof is already being worked on is significantly more cost effective than addressing them separately later.</p>\n<p>Finally the accessibility of your specific site affects cost. Properties with difficult access due to height, steep driveways, proximity to boundaries or other site specific constraints require additional safety setup and equipment which is reflected in the overall price.</p>\n<h3><b>Residential Roofing Auckland: Material Options and How They Affect Cost</b></h3>\n<p>The roofing material you choose for your Auckland home has one of the biggest impacts on the overall cost of the project. Understanding the main options available and how they compare helps you make a more informed decision when you are weighing up quotes.</p>\n<p>Long run steel roofing is one of the most widely used residential roofing materials in Auckland and has been for decades. It is durable, relatively lightweight and available in a wide range of profiles and colours. Long run steel suits homes with lower roof pitches particularly well and gives a clean contemporary appearance that works with many Auckland architectural styles. It is generally one of the more cost effective options per square metre which makes it a popular choice for homeowners who want reliable long term performance without paying a premium price.</p>\n<p>Colorsteel is New Zealand’s most recognised and trusted steel roofing product and represents a step up from standard long run steel in terms of coating quality and corrosion resistance. It is specifically engineered for New Zealand’s coastal and urban environments and is backed by a manufacturer warranty that reflects confidence in its long term performance. For Auckland homes where salt air, humidity and UV exposure are ongoing factors Colorsteel is a particularly well suited choice. The additional upfront cost compared to standard steel is generally well justified over the lifespan of the roof.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/concrete-tile-roofing/\">Concrete tile roofing</a> has been a fixture of New Zealand residential architecture for decades and remains a popular choice for homeowners who prefer the traditional aesthetic of a tiled roof. Concrete tiles are heavy, durable and provide good thermal mass which contributes to the comfort of the home throughout the year. They require a roof structure capable of supporting their weight which is something a roofing contractor will assess during the initial inspection. If your home already has an old concrete tile roof, we specialise in replacing it with a lighter, modern long-run COLORSTEEL® or metal tile roof.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/metal-tile-roofing/\">Metal tile roofing</a> has grown steadily in popularity across Auckland in recent years. Metal tiles replicate the visual appearance of traditional concrete or terracotta tiles convincingly while offering the lighter weight and long term durability of steel. They are a particularly popular choice when replacing an existing concrete tile roof where a lighter cladding option is needed, or when a homeowner wants the look of a tiled roof without the structural demands of concrete. The combination of visual appeal and practical performance makes metal tiles one of the more versatile residential roofing options available in Auckland.</p>\n<p>When comparing materials it is worth thinking beyond just the upfront cost per square metre. The expected lifespan of the product, the manufacturer warranty, how well it suits the existing structure of your home and how it looks against your overall property aesthetic are all worth factoring into the decision.</p>\n<h3><b>Realistic Cost Ranges for Residential Roofing in Auckland</b></h3>\n<p>Without seeing your specific property it is not possible to give you a precise figure for residential roofing costs in Auckland. Every home is different and accurate pricing requires a proper site assessment. However understanding the general price ranges helps you plan and budget before you start the formal quoting process.</p>\n<p>For a smaller single storey home using long run steel or Colorsteel roofing, residential roofing costs in Auckland typically cost around $12,000 to $18,000 for a roof under 100m², and around $16,000 to $28,000 for a standard home with a roof of 100 to 180m². This covers the removal and disposal of the existing roof, supply and installation of the new cladding, replacement of flashings and roofline elements where required and the workmanship warranty.</p>\n<p>For larger or more complex properties the cost extends significantly. A large two storey home with a complex roof shape, multiple penetrations and significant roofline work can cost $25,000 to $40,000 or more depending on the specific scope of work involved.</p>\n<p>Metal tile roofing projects, including replacing an old concrete tile roof, are priced after a site visit, with the cost determined by the size and complexity of the roof and the products selected.</p>\n<p>These figures are general guides only. The only reliable way to know what your specific project will cost is to have a qualified roofing contractor visit your property, inspect the existing roof thoroughly and provide a detailed quote covering the full scope of work.</p>\n<p>A note on low quotes: if a quote comes in significantly below the general market range it is worth asking detailed questions about what is included, what products are being specified and whether the contractor is properly insured and warranting their workmanship. A roof replacement is a major investment and the consequences of a poorly done job are significant and costly to remedy.</p>\n<h3><b>Roof Replacement Auckland: What Is Included in a Quality Quote</b></h3>\n<p>Understanding what should be included in a residential roofing quote helps you compare quotes accurately and identify any that may be leaving important work out of scope.</p>\n<p>A comprehensive residential roofing quote should include the removal and disposal of your existing roof cladding. Some contractors quote for installation only and charge separately for removal and disposal which can add meaningfully to the final cost. Confirming this upfront avoids surprises.</p>\n<p>The supply and installation of the new roofing material should be clearly specified including the product name, profile and colour selected. This allows you to compare quotes on a like for like basis rather than assuming different contractors are quoting the same product when they may not be.</p>\n<p>Replacement of flashings around all penetrations, edges and junctions should be included as standard. Reusing existing flashings on a new roof is poor practice that compromises the weathertightness of the installation and should not be accepted.</p>\n<p>Guttering, spouting and fascia replacement or reinstallation should be addressed in the quote. Some projects require full replacement of roofline elements while others may only require reinstatement. Either way this should be clearly covered.</p>\n<p>A workmanship warranty of meaningful duration should be clearly stated in the quote documentation. This is your protection against installation defects and any reputable roofing company should provide this without hesitation.</p>\n<p>Site cleanup and disposal of all waste materials should be included. A professional roofing crew leaves your property in the same condition it was in before the project began with the only difference being a new roof.</p>\n<h3><b>Roofing Company Auckland: What to Look for Before You Commit</b></h3>\n<p>Choosing the <a href=\"/\">right roofing company for your Auckland residential roofing project</a> is just as important as choosing the right material. The quality of the installation determines how your new roof performs over its lifetime and an experienced, professional crew makes an enormous difference to the final result.</p>\n<p>A roofing company with a strong track record of completed residential projects across Auckland and verifiable customer reviews gives you real evidence of the standard of their work. Ask to see examples of completed projects on homes similar to yours and pay attention to how the company communicates from your very first interaction.</p>\n<p>Experience in the Auckland market specifically matters. A company that has been working on Auckland homes for a significant period understands the specific conditions, architectural styles and challenges that Auckland residential roofing presents. This local knowledge shows up in the quality of their assessment, the relevance of their material recommendations and the care they take during installation.</p>\n<p>Proper insurance coverage is non-negotiable. Any roofing company working on your property should carry full public liability insurance and have appropriate cover for their workers. Confirming this before the project begins protects you from any liability in the event of an accident or incident on site.</p>\n<p>Clear and consistent communication throughout the project is one of the things homeowners most consistently value in a roofing contractor. From the initial assessment through to the final inspection a contractor who keeps you informed, explains what they are doing and addresses any questions or concerns promptly makes the whole experience significantly less stressful.</p>\n<h3><b>Making the Right Decision for Your Auckland Home</b></h3>\n<p>Residential roofing costs in Auckland are significant but so is the value of getting the project right. A quality new roof protects your home for decades, adds real value to your property and eliminates the ongoing cost and stress of a roof that is past its useful life.</p>\n<p>The most important steps before committing to any residential roofing project are getting a thorough professional assessment of your existing roof, understanding what your material options are and what each one means for your budget and your home, and <a href=\"/about/\">choosing a roofing company with the experience</a>, track record and professionalism to deliver a result that will last.</p>\n<p>Understanding what drives residential roofing costs in Auckland puts you in a much stronger position to make confident decisions throughout the process and ensures the investment you make in your roof delivers the return it should for years to come.</p>"
  },
  {
   "slug": "re-roofing-guide",
   "title": "Re-roofing Guide",
   "date": "2021-06-30",
   "image": "blog-reroofing-guide.jpg",
   "excerpt": "Why you need to re-roof your house might be out of necessity, wanting a change or redesigning. Your roof maybe failing, loosing colour, corrosion, rust, mould or sprung leaks. What ever the reason you are looking at re-roofing we have",
   "content": "<p>Why you need to re-roof your house might be out of necessity, wanting a change or redesigning. Your roof maybe failing, loosing colour, corrosion, rust, mould or sprung leaks. What ever the reason you are looking at re-roofing we have put together a few things to help you along the journey.</p>\n<p>Thinking about the cost to re-roof your home it is important to look at the long term cost. Cost for re-roofing can be broken down in to two sections, the up-front costs (materials and installation) and the longevity costs. Using cheap materials and/or less than reputable roofer may mean you save money in the short term but can mean that you end up having to replace your roof in only a few years.</p>\n<p>Things you need to consider when you are re-roofing:</p>\n<ul><li>Make sure if you are re-roofing due to existing roof fail, that you talk to us about working out what caused it. We will then get rid of the existing problem(s) before beginning.</li><li>Ask us to assess whether it is necessary to tear off the existing shingles/tiles before re-roofing. If it is necessary we will dispose of them for you.</li><li>Check to see if you roof is properly ventilated</li><li>Check your insulation, do you need to upgrade, easier to install when the roof is off.</li><li>Does your roof need underlayment</li><li>Check the roof framing structure, rafters, beams, purlins etc, we can check these to see if they need replacing</li><li>Check your spoutings, down pipes and other rainwater systems, we can also help with this and advise</li><li>What is the climate conditions the roof will be exposed to</li><li>The roof shape and profile</li><li>What product is best for your home, we can advise you on what your options are</li><li>What colour do you want</li></ul>\n<p>Once you have decided on your new roof these are the 10 steps to re-roofing:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Set up</strong>: scaffolding/edge protection</li><li><strong>Remove</strong>: old tiles/roofing iron</li><li><strong>Inspect</strong>: check the roof structure for defects</li><li><strong>Prepare</strong>: roof surface will be prepared (drip edge, underlay) and if needed install insulation</li><li><strong>Install</strong>: new battens or purlins if necessary</li><li><strong>Load up</strong>: roofing materials</li><li><strong>Lay</strong>: roofing products</li><li><strong>Lay</strong>: ridge tiles/install flashings</li><li><strong>Pointing</strong>: nailing/rivets and sealents</li><li><strong>Finish</strong>: site clean up and final inspection.</li></ol>\n<p>Talk to the us today about getting the process started, and we can help you through your decisions to get the roof that is build to last.</p>\n<h2>Products for your new roof</h2>\n<h3>COLORSTEEL® ROOFING</h3>\n<p>The modern Colorsteel® versions of this classic, cost effective, long-run steel offer greater strength and durability, and a huge range of colours and profiles. There’s a Colorsteel® solution for almost any situation. All products are designed to perform across a wide range of environments and job types.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/colour-steel-roofing/\">More information</a></p>\n<h3>METAL TILE ROOFING</h3>\n<p>If you’re wanting the look of a traditional tiled roof, but one that is lighter and more durable, then a Gerard metal tile roof may be your answer. Gerard metal tiles come in a range of profiles and colours, and we're resellers and installers of Gerard products.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/metal-tile-roofing/\">More information</a></p>\n<h3>LONG-RUN ROOFING</h3>\n<p>Long-run roofing, metal roofing or corrugated iron as it’s more widely known, has long been a staple building material in New Zealand. Classic, versatile and born for our climate, no wonder long-run is still one of the most popular roofing style’s in New Zealand.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/colour-steel-roofing/\">More information</a></p>\n<h3>CONCRETE TILE ROOFING</h3>\n<p>Durable, strong and non-corrosive, these roof tiles will withstand the most extreme conditions New Zealand weather can throw at them. Tiles offer a diversity that other roofing materials simply can’t match. They come in an extensive range of profiles to suit any style of architecture.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/concrete-tile-roofing/\">More information</a></p>"
  },
  {
   "slug": "is-tile-roof-replacement-right-for-your-home",
   "title": "Is Tile Roof Replacement Right for Your Home?",
   "date": "2025-10-23",
   "image": "blog-tile-roof.jpg",
   "excerpt": "When it comes to maintaining the structural integrity and aesthetic appeal of your home, the roof plays a crucial role. Over time, even the most robust roofing materials will require attention, and for homeowners with tile roofs, a tile roof",
   "content": "<p>When it comes to maintaining the structural integrity and aesthetic appeal of your home, the roof plays a crucial role. Over time, even the most robust roofing materials will require attention, and for homeowners with tile roofs, a tile roof replacement may become necessary. But is it the best solution for your home? <br> In this post, we’ll explore everything you need to know about tile roof replacement, from its benefits to the key factors you should consider.</p>\n<h3><b>What is a Tile Roof Replacement?</b></h3>\n<p>A tile roof replacement involves removing existing roofing tiles and installing new ones. This process not only revitalises the appearance of your home but also ensures that your roof continues to function efficiently. Tiles can be made from various materials, including metal, clay, concrete, and slate, each offering distinct characteristics and benefits.</p>\n<h3><b>Why Consider a Tile Roof Replacement?</b></h3>\n<p>Tile roofs are renowned for their durability, longevity, and timeless appeal. However, even the most resilient materials may eventually wear out due to exposure to harsh weather conditions, such as heavy rainfall, strong winds, or extreme heat. A timely roof replacement can help you avoid potential issues like leaks or structural damage. Additionally, a tile roof replacement can also enhance your home’s energy efficiency. Modern tiles are designed to reflect solar heat, helping to regulate your home’s temperature and potentially reducing energy costs.</p>\n<h3><b>Signs You Need a Tile Roof Replacement</b></h3>\n<p>To determine if a tile roof replacement is necessary, look for these common signs:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Cracked, Broken or Dented Tiles:</strong> Damaged tiles can lead to water leaks and further deterioration if not addressed promptly.</li><li><strong>Moss and Algae Growth:</strong> Excessive growth can indicate moisture retention and potential damage to the underlying structure.</li><li><strong>Sagging Roof:</strong> A sagging roof can be a sign of structural issues that need immediate attention.</li><li><strong>Frequent Repairs:</strong> If you find yourself constantly repairing your tile roof, it might be more cost-effective to opt for a total replacement.</li></ul>\n<h3><b>Types of Tile Materials</b></h3>\n<p>Understanding the types of tile materials available can help you make an informed decision about your roof replacement.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Clay Tiles:</strong> Known for their classic look and long lifespan, clay tiles are resistant to colour fading and maintain their beauty over time.</li><li><strong>Concrete Tiles:</strong> More affordable than clay, concrete tiles are highly durable and available in a wide range of styles and colours.</li><li><strong>Metal Tiles:</strong> Lightweight and long-lasting, metal tiles offer excellent durability with low maintenance and come in a variety of modern styles and colours.</li></ul>\n<h3><b>Considering the Costs</b></h3>\n<p>The cost of a tile roof replacement can vary significantly depending on several factors:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Material Choice:</strong> Clay and slate tiles can be more expensive than concrete and metal tile options.</li><li><strong>Roof Size and Complexity:</strong> Larger and more complex roofs require more materials and labour.</li><li><strong>Removal of Old Tiles:</strong> Labour costs may increase if the removal process is challenging. While replacing a tile roof can be a considerable investment, it is often offset by the increased value and protection it provides to your home.</li></ul>\n<h3><b>Tile Roof Replacement Process</b></h3>\n<p>The process of replacing a tile roof involves several key steps: Inspection: A thorough inspection of your existing roof will identify areas that need attention and ensure proper planning.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Removal:</strong> The old tiles are carefully removed, and any damaged underlying structures are repaired or replaced.</li><li><strong>Installation:</strong> New tiles are installed, ensuring proper alignment and sealing to prevent future issues.</li><li><strong>Final Inspection:</strong> A final inspection ensures that the roof meets quality standards and that everything is securely in place.</li></ol>\n<h3><b>FAQ</b></h3>\n<h4><strong>How long does a tile roof last?</strong></h4>\n<p>Tile roofs can last anywhere from 50 to 100 years, depending on the material and maintenance. Regular inspections and maintenance can help extend their lifespan.</p>\n<h4><strong>Can I replace my tile roof with a different material?</strong></h4>\n<p>Yes, you can switch to a different roofing material, but it’s important to consider the weight and structural requirements. <a href=\"/\">Consult with a roofing professional</a> to determine the best option for your home.</p>\n<h4><strong>Are tile roofs weather-resistant?</strong></h4>\n<p>Tile roofs are highly weather-resistant and are excellent at withstanding various climate conditions, including intense sun, heavy rain, and high winds.</p>\n<h3><b>Conclusion</b></h3>\n<p>Considering a tile roof replacement is a significant decision that can greatly impact the function and appearance of your home. By understanding the benefits, signs of wear, and the replacement process, you can make an informed choice that aligns with your needs and budget.</p>\n<p>If you’re ready to <a href=\"/services/reroofing/\">explore a tile roof replacement</a>, contact our expert team today.</p>\n<p>We offer comprehensive roofing services tailored to your home’s unique requirements, ensuring quality workmanship and lasting results.</p>\n<p>Transform your home with a beautiful, durable tile roof that stands the test of time.</p>"
  },
  {
   "slug": "maintenance-cleaning-for-your-metrotile-roof",
   "title": "MAINTENANCE & CLEANING For Your Metrotile Roof",
   "date": "2021-06-30",
   "image": "blog-metrotile-care.jpg",
   "excerpt": "Metrotile and other pressed metal roofing tiles require minor maintenance to ensure the continued performance of the roof over its life. Where possible a visual ground inspection of your roof should be performed every year, or after any significant storm,",
   "content": "<p><em><strong>First published on Metrotile <a href=\"https://www.metrotile.com/news-blogs/caring-for-your-roof-maintenance-cleaning/\">website</a></strong></em></p>\n<h2>Maintenance</h2>\n<p>Metrotile and other pressed metal roofing tiles require minor maintenance to ensure the continued performance of the roof over its life. Where possible a visual ground inspection of your roof should be performed every year, or after any significant storm, for the following items.</p>\n<p>Maintenance or repairs to the following items should help prevent any major problems occurring:</p>\n<ul><li>Damaged or displaced tiles or flashings.</li><li>Blocked valleys, spouting or downpipes with debris such as leaves, other plant matter or foreign objects such as toys.</li><li>Wash down any roof areas not effected by rain washing (see Cleaning below).</li></ul>\n<p>In certain environmental conditions moss, mould or lichen may present itself on areas of the roof. To maintain the integrity of the coating and for aesthetic reasons this can easily be removed with a generic roof wash treatment, such as Mossboss, applied by low pressure spraying. When the roof is used for collection of potable water, ensure that the downpipes are disconnected for the period stated in the spraying instructions. Failure to maintain the roof in this manner will void the coating warranty.</p>\n<p>When repairs or maintenance are needed we recommend that you consult a metal tile roofing specialist, as additional damage to the roof tiles can be caused by inexperienced foot traffic (If you must access the roof follow these instructions, to avoid denting or damaging the roof tiles, you should wear soft sole shoes and place your foot on the lowest point of the tile and at the front edge of the tile). Also, house holders unfamiliar with working at height pose considerable risk of injury to themselves by falling from ladders or the roof</p>\n<h2>Cleaning</h2>\n<p>Metrotile roofs must be washed down regularly with fresh water in all areas not washed by rain, such as those areas sheltered by the eaves overhang of a higher roof. In areas near the sea, where salt deposition is noticeable on windows and similar surfaces, or near areas of industrial pollution, washing down should be carried out every 2-3 months. In other areas, washing down every 6 months should suffice.</p>\n<p>Lichen and mould growth must be removed with chemical cleaners such as Mossboss. We recommend to chemically clean the roof once every 2-4 years to maintain a good appearance and prevent the growth of algae, moss and lichen. Failure to carry out this maintenance program may void the coating warranty.</p>"
  },
  {
   "slug": "caring-for-colorsteel",
   "title": "Caring for COLORSTEEL®",
   "date": "2021-06-30",
   "image": "blog-colorsteel-care.jpg",
   "excerpt": "COLORSTEEL® products are made to be durable and low maintenance, but they still need to be looked after. Here are a few tips to build into your maintenance routine.",
   "content": "<p><em><strong>Sourced from Colorsteel <a href=\"https://www.colorsteel.co.nz/caring-for-colorsteel/\">website</a></strong></em></p>\n<p>COLORSTEEL® products are made to be durable and low maintenance, but they still need to be looked after. Here are a few tips to build into your maintenance routine.</p>\n<p>If you’re looking for specific maintenance requirements for a Colorsteel product, check out the warranty and maintenance page.</p>\n<h2>The limits of rain</h2>\n<p>Rain plays an important role in keeping your COLORSTEEL® products in good condition, but there are some places where it can’t reach. You need to clean these areas yourself, to ensure debris doesn’t build up. Cleaning your COLORSTEEL® product manually will increase the steel’s longevity, as it reduces attacks from salt and other pollutants in the air. It’s also an important part of keeping your COLORSTEEL® warranty valid.</p>\n<h2>Preventing serious issues</h2>\n<h4>Keep an eye on tree fall and debris</h4>\n<p>If you have a garden, leaves, twigs, dust and dirt can get blown up onto your roof. You’ll need to manually remove anything left over to ensure no damage is caused. Remove any build up with a soft bristled brush every three months.</p>\n<h4>Monitor high risk areas</h4>\n<p>If not washed properly, the places where the rain can’t reach can cause serious problems.</p>\n<p>Areas that require a more extensive clean:</p>\n<ul><li>Soffits</li><li>Wall cladding under eaves</li><li>Underside of gutters</li><li>Fascias</li><li>Sheltered areas of garage doors and unwashed roof areas</li><li>Around flues</li><li>Under television aerials</li><li>Sites prone to mould, lichen, bird droppings or debris.</li></ul>\n<h4>Clear your gutters</h4>\n<p>Gutters can easily become clogged by leaves, twigs and other debris. This can cause a number of problems around your home such as a leaky roof, and mould and mildew. Clear your gutters every three months to ensure they function correctly.</p>\n<h4>Watch out for branches rubbing against your roof</h4>\n<p>Branches and foliage that come into contact with your house can do significant damage to your roofing or cladding. Prune back any potentially damaging plants every three months.</p>\n<h2>Catching issues early</h2>\n<p>Although regular roof maintenance will significantly reduce the chances of damage, conditions on your property can change. Examine your roof at least twice a year, this will allow you to respond to any issues before they become more serious.</p>\n<h4>Inside your home and ceiling cavity look out for:</h4>\n<ul><li>Places where the ceiling is sagging</li><li>Signs of water damage or leaking</li><li>Dark spots that might indicate mould</li><li>Outside light peeking through.</li></ul>\n<h4>Outside your home look out for:</h4>\n<ul><li>Fastenings that appear loose</li><li>Roof attachments that appear loose e.g. satellite dish, solar heating unit or conventional television aerials.</li><li>Clogged or loose gutters and downpipes.</li></ul>"
  }
 ],
